course: dictionary
lesson: 5380
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Knight has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one is meant. «Their best fighters were knights, rich horsemen trained from boyhood, who wore heavy armour» has clues: horsemen, armour and fighters. So a knight here is a soldier from the Middle Ages who fought on horseback.
explain: Knight can also be an honour. In the UK today, the King or Queen can knight a man for great service, and he is then called «Sir». Look at the words around it to see whether the text is about history, honours or chess.
explain: Knight and night sound the same, but they are spelled differently. Ava reads «The knight rode at night» and frowns. The meaning tells her which spelling to write. Check the sense of the sentence, not just the sound.
explain: A knight can also be a figure of speech. «A knight in shining armour» means someone who comes to rescue another person. Nobody is actually wearing armour here: the phrase is describing a hero.
fact: In the Middle Ages, a knight was a soldier who fought on horseback in armour and served a king, queen or lord.
fact: «Chivalry was the code knights were meant to follow: be brave and loyal, protect the weak and honour God.»
fact: «Coats of arms let knights be recognised when helmets hid their faces.»
fact: In the UK today, a knight is a man given an honour by the King or Queen, and «to knight someone» means to give them this honour.
fact: In chess, the knight is shaped like a horse's head and moves in an L shape.
exercise: «Their best fighters were knights, rich horsemen trained from boyhood, who wore heavy armour.» What is a knight in this sentence? | *A soldier who fought on horseback in armour | A dark part of the day | A horse that carries the king's messages | A kind of chess board
exercise: Ava reads «The knight rode at night» and frowns. Why? | Knight is a colour and night is a kind of armour | They mean exactly the same thing | *They sound the same but are spelled differently | Night is a word for a horse
exercise: «Coats of arms let knights be recognised when helmets hid their faces.» What did coats of arms help people do? | Keep warm in winter | *Recognise who the knight was | Ride a horse faster | Cook a meal
test: «They painted coats of arms on their shields so friends could tell who was who in battle.» What were the coats of arms for? | Hiding from enemies in the woods | Decorating a castle kitchen | Making the shield lighter | *Showing who the knight was
test: In the UK today, what does «to knight someone» mean? | To make them a chess piece | To move them in an L shape | To fight them on horseback | *To give them an honour and call them Sir
exam: «A knight in shining armour» means what? | A chess player who always wins | *Someone who comes to rescue another person | A shiny metal suit for sale in a shop | A very fast horse
